      </div>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-esc">Escalation path:</strong> F6 (62%) plus Iran's established retaliation pattern across the Sep 1–2 exchange drives continued US-Iran kinetic cycling with no diplomatic circuit-breaker in range.</p>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-prot">Protracted path:</strong> CENTCOM's claimed "month of lowered threat" from Sep 1 strike package plus Iran's message-relay engagement produces a de facto pause without a formal ceasefire or session (F3 at 10%).</p>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-desc">De-escalation path:</strong> Iran's public MOU reciprocation offer — if accepted by Washington before Sep 6 (F2, 8%) — could open a compressed diplomatic lane, but Trump's Sep 2 waveoff makes this a low-probability pathway.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ========== 5. INSIDE IRAN (descriptive, ~200 words) ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="inside-iran">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      Inside Iran
      <span class="meta">State-media decode · 200 words · verb-ban + forecast-cite rule applies</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="inside-iran">
      <div class="iran-media-badges">
        <span class="iran-media-badge">IRNA</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">IRIB</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Press TV</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Fars</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Tasnim</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Kayhan</span>
      </div>
      <p>Iranian state media covered the September 1–2 exchange under a uniform "defensive operations" frame. IRNA quoted IRGC commander Hossein Salami calling the ballistic missile strikes on Jordan and Kuwait bases "a natural response to aggression against Iranian sovereignty." Tasnim carried a translated version of Araghchi's letter to the UN Security Council, foregrounding Article 51 self-defense language. Kayhan ran a front-page editorial declaring the "era of strategic patience is over" and called for expanded targeting of US logistics infrastructure. Press TV broadcast footage described as missile launches and credited the IRGC Aerospace Force without independent verification. No Iranian state outlet acknowledged military casualties from the Sirik wedding strike; civilian death claims were attributed to "enemy propaganda" by Tasnim (Sep 1). → <span class="context-only">context only</span></p>
      <p>Araghchi's public output maintained two simultaneous tracks: a UN condemnation letter condemning the Sep 1 strikes and a separate SNSC statement offering cessation of operations if US attacks stop. This dual-channel pattern — legal condemnation plus a preconditioned de-escalatory offer — has appeared in previous Iranian diplomatic phases without leading to a convened session. Neither IRNA nor Tasnim quoted any official committing to mediated talks without conditions. → supports <span class="cite">F2</span> without move</p>
    </div>
  </div>

